<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Privileged-permission allowlist for the IRA CARE watch app (package care.ira.watch).
  Installed to /product/etc/permissions/ by Android.bp (prebuilt_etc).

  UNBUILT: not yet read by any running system (docs/os/BUILD.md).

  This list is deliberately EMPTY, because the app's manifest
  (watch/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml; the aosp flavour adds no manifest of its own)
  requests no permission whose protection level includes "privileged". What it requests:

    normal:      INTERNET, FOREGROUND_SERVICE, FOREGROUND_SERVICE_HEALTH, VIBRATE,
                 WAKE_LOCK, RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED
    dangerous:   POST_NOTIFICATIONS, RECORD_AUDIO, BODY_SENSORS, BODY_SENSORS_BACKGROUND,
                 ACTIVITY_RECOGNITION, ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION, ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION

  Dangerous (runtime) permissions are not granted by this file; the first-boot grants are
  proposed in default-permissions-care.ira.watch.xml. If the app ever requests a
  signature|privileged permission (for example REBOOT or WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS), it must
  be added here in the same change, or the device refuses to boot with
  ro.control_privapp_permissions=enforce. The readiness test
  supracloud-jarvis/ira/tests/readiness/test_os_product_layer.py fails until both agree.

  The list is derived from the source manifest. Libraries can add permissions when Gradle
  merges manifests (WorkManager, for example, adds normal ones such as
  ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE); the build host checks the built APK with
  `aapt2 dump permissions` before it is imported (os/scripts/build_cuttlefish.sh).

  Copyright 2026 IRA CARE. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
-->
<permissions>
    <privapp-permissions package="care.ira.watch">
    </privapp-permissions>
</permissions>
